1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,007
Hello, and welcome to this short demo of
our file transfer app.

2
00:00:05,007 --> 00:00:09,776
The idea behind it is simple: you have a
file on one device,

3
00:00:09,776 --> 00:00:15,022
and you want it on another, and this app
lets you do exactly that,

4
00:00:15,022 --> 00:00:18,837
in just a few clicks. Let me show you the
setup.

5
00:00:18,837 --> 00:00:21,380
On the left is the first device.

6
00:00:21,380 --> 00:00:24,321
This one runs the application itself,

7
00:00:24,321 --> 00:00:28,851
it acts as both the server and a client
at the same time.

8
00:00:28,851 --> 00:00:31,951
On the right side is the second device.

9
00:00:31,951 --> 00:00:34,494
It doesn't run anything special.

10
00:00:34,494 --> 00:00:39,819
It simply connects to the first device
over the same Wi-Fi network.

11
00:00:39,819 --> 00:00:43,714
And you can actually see this in the
address bar:

12
00:00:43,714 --> 00:00:46,496
it's the first device's IP address,

13
00:00:46,496 --> 00:00:50,470
followed by the port of our file transfer
service.

14
00:00:50,470 --> 00:00:55,000
That's all it takes to join. Now, let's
see it in action.

15
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:57,583
Let's start on the first device.

16
00:00:57,583 --> 00:01:01,619
I'll pick a file that I want to share,
here it is.

17
00:01:01,619 --> 00:01:07,108
Now I simply click Send. And just like
that, the file is on the way.

18
00:01:07,108 --> 00:01:08,964
It only takes a moment,

19
00:01:08,964 --> 00:01:13,000
and the app confirms that everything went
through.

20
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:18,800
Because the file is now on the server,

21
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:25,500
we can see that both devices have access
to the file that have been

22
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:26,400
uploaded,

23
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:31,500
and can both perform the actions that are
possible.

24
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:35,000
That means delete or download them.

25
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:39,788
Let's move over to the second device and
click Download,

26
00:01:39,788 --> 00:01:44,320
and the file is saved locally, right
where I want it.

27
00:01:44,320 --> 00:01:49,364
And there it is: exactly the same file,
with the same name,

28
00:01:49,364 --> 00:01:53,297
now sitting on a completely different
machine.

29
00:01:53,297 --> 00:01:58,000
That's the whole purpose of the app, and
it just works.

30
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:04,309
Finally, let's clean things up. Once a
file has served its purpose,

31
00:02:04,309 --> 00:02:08,192
there's no reason to keep it around. I
select it,

32
00:02:08,192 --> 00:02:12,234
and I click Delete. The file is removed
right away,

33
00:02:12,234 --> 00:02:14,691
and because the list is shared,

34
00:02:14,691 --> 00:02:20,000
it disappears from both devices at the
same time. Simple, and tidy.

35
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:26,077
And that's the full journey: we sent a
file from the first device,

36
00:02:26,077 --> 00:02:30,154
watched it appear on the second,
downloaded it there,

37
00:02:30,154 --> 00:02:31,923
and finally deleted it,

38
00:02:31,923 --> 00:02:36,923
all between two real machines talking to
each other over the same

39
00:02:36,923 --> 00:02:37,538
network.

40
00:02:37,538 --> 00:02:41,000
This wraps up the first phase of our
project.

41
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:43,000
Thanks a lot for watching.
